00:28:06,680 --> 00:28:10,140
Rym: The good one that I was really looking forward to doing that panel.

397
00:28:10,720 --> 00:28:15,100
Scott: Well, I'm likely to you know, my work is having, you know, big basically everyone.

398
00:28:15,260 --> 00:28:17,500
Scott: My work is entirely remote with people all over the world.

399
00:28:17,841 --> 00:28:19,788
Scott: And last year they were like, all right, everyone.

400
00:28:19,828 --> 00:28:23,420
Scott: in September, we're going to get everyone in the whole company together in New York.

401
00:28:23,500 --> 00:28:24,001
Scott: I'm like, yes.

402
00:28:24,964 --> 00:28:31,440
Scott: And this year they're like reading everyone at the company together in exactly one week after PAX West in California.

403
00:28:31,460 --> 00:28:33,266
Scott: And I'm like, well, I'll be over there.

404
00:28:34,951 --> 00:28:37,600
Scott: I'll just find a range of transportation of some kind.

405
00:28:37,620 --> 00:28:39,627
Rym: You know, you could do if you want to have some fun.

406
00:28:39,647 --> 00:28:43,300
Rym: I would recommend taking that scenic Amtrak.

407
00:28:43,921 --> 00:28:44,709
Scott: We'll see who pays.

408
00:28:44,729 --> 00:28:45,700
Scott: That's what we'll see.

409
00:28:45,860 --> 00:28:46,002
Rym: Yeah.

410
00:28:46,042 --> 00:28:47,540
Rym: Take that down the coast because I took that.

411
00:28:47,640 --> 00:28:50,420
Scott: Well see, it's all going to depend on who pays for what, right?

412
00:28:50,480 --> 00:28:51,358
Scott: Because I took one year.

413
00:28:51,780 --> 00:28:53,980
Scott: If my company doesn't pay, I'm just going right back to New York.

414
00:28:54,220 --> 00:29:08,900
Rym: In ancient, ancient times, there was a PAX West where an organization, Kai-Fu, approached me and paid me my honorarium and all my travel expenses to travel after PAX to Portland to give a private lecture on game and UX design to this group.

415
00:29:09,000 --> 00:29:09,702
Rym: And it was super fun.

416
00:29:10,284 --> 00:29:15,057
Rym: So I took the Amtrak from Seattle to Portland and I really, really enjoyed that.

417
00:29:15,097 --> 00:29:16,020
Rym: Like that was a great time.

418
00:29:16,284 --> 00:29:17,138
Rym: So I would recommend it.

419
00:29:17,440 --> 00:29:25,020
Scott: I mean, I do have many coworkers in Portland, so that is an option is to go to where, you know, several coworkers, including the CEO, are in Portland.

420
00:29:25,481 --> 00:29:29,820
Scott: So I could go there and then go with them to California, right?

421
00:29:30,122 --> 00:29:30,536
Rym: Could be fun.

422
00:29:31,281 --> 00:29:32,039
Scott: That's one option.

423
00:29:32,200 --> 00:29:34,567
Rym: Well, very likely also be at the PAX Unplugged.

424
00:29:34,587 --> 00:29:35,851
Rym: That'll happen sometime after that.

425
00:29:36,393 --> 00:29:38,600
Rym: We'll pretty much be at PAX unless things get in our way.

426
00:29:39,141 --> 00:29:49,700
Rym: I'll also tell you all now, I am strongly debating not just going to MagFest, as we do every year, but going to MagStock in June.

427
00:29:50,821 --> 00:29:54,980
Rym: It actually looks pretty fun the more I looked into it and I'm going to make a decision in the next couple weeks.

428
00:29:56,261 --> 00:30:02,119
Scott: So, you know, it's like I could see some good parts, but I could also see potential for meh.

429
00:30:02,660 --> 00:30:04,340
Rym: So there's important things I learned.

430
00:30:04,822 --> 00:30:07,351
Rym: One, everyone has access to showers.

431
00:30:07,512 --> 00:30:08,817
Rym: Even people are staying in tents.

432
00:30:09,098 --> 00:30:09,620
Rym: That was the first.

433
00:30:10,365 --> 00:30:11,560
Scott: That's not something I'm worried about.

434
00:30:11,840 --> 00:30:14,060
Rym: That was the first and most important thing I was worried about.

435
00:30:14,583 --> 00:30:14,772
Scott: Okay.

436
00:30:16,901 --> 00:30:22,840
Rym: It leans more into the concerts, but they do have like powered gaming areas and such.

437
00:30:23,520 --> 00:30:23,609
Scott: Yeah.

438
00:30:24,621 --> 00:30:29,375
Rym: But what I was really interested in is if you're in a cabin, the cabins aren't actually that expensive.

439
00:30:29,455 --> 00:30:30,960
Rym: Like if you don't want to camp in a tent.

440
00:30:31,500 --> 00:30:33,660
Scott: If you can fill the cabin with people, it's not that expensive.

441
00:30:33,760 --> 00:30:33,981
Rym: Yeah.

442
00:30:34,041 --> 00:30:41,197
Rym: So the largest kind of private cabin you can get costs $1,400 for the whole con and sleeps.

443
00:30:41,257 --> 00:30:42,179
Rym: Guess how many it sleeps?

444
00:30:43,225 --> 00:30:44,119
Scott: Probably like teens.

445
00:30:45,791 --> 00:30:45,979
Scott: Okay.

446
00:30:46,140 --> 00:30:47,098
Scott: It has thirty beds.

447
00:30:48,049 --> 00:30:48,300
Scott: Okay.

448
00:30:49,161 --> 00:30:49,983
Rym: So stay tuned.

449
00:30:50,003 --> 00:30:50,785
Rym: I'm going to look into this.

450
00:30:51,066 --> 00:30:56,300
Rym: I'll give you, I'll share with all of you my assessment and my decision on whether or not I want to go to MagStock.

451
00:30:56,501 --> 00:30:59,179
Rym: And then if other people want to go, we'll see what happens.

452
00:31:01,602 --> 00:31:03,560
Rym: Otherwise, yeah, we're going to try not to disrupt.

453
00:31:03,600 --> 00:31:06,340
Scott: I figured you would just use your tent and not pay anything.

454
00:31:06,720 --> 00:31:07,383
Rym: Could do that too.

455
00:31:07,464 --> 00:31:10,920
Rym: But I really like camping one or two nights in a tent.

456
00:31:11,440 --> 00:31:17,000
Rym: I don't know if I want to camp in a tent during a gaming convention and a music festival for a long weekend.

457
00:31:17,700 --> 00:31:19,248
Rym: I feel like I might want to be in a cabin though.

458
00:31:19,268 --> 00:31:21,720
Rym: If you do camp, the rules are pretty cool.

459
00:31:22,140 --> 00:31:23,686
Rym: You can camp anywhere on the ground.

460
00:31:23,806 --> 00:31:25,291
Rym: It isn't like designated campsites.

461
00:31:25,332 --> 00:31:27,600
Rym: Just pick a spot that's yours.

462
00:31:28,521 --> 00:31:31,960
Rym: But everyone has access to showers and facilities.

463
00:31:33,881 --> 00:31:38,060
Rym: They even have a whole system to make sure you have places to get power, even run power to your tent if you want.

464
00:31:38,723 --> 00:31:39,820
Scott: What's the food situation?

465
00:31:40,520 --> 00:31:43,620
Rym: There is a dining hall that you can opt into or cook your own food.

466
00:31:45,021 --> 00:31:45,527
Scott: Okay.

467
00:31:45,567 --> 00:31:46,559
Scott: How many days is it though?

468
00:31:47,081 --> 00:31:47,803
Rym: I think three or four.

469
00:31:47,823 --> 00:31:50,009
Rym: I have mostly focused on the.

470
00:31:50,089 --> 00:31:53,820
Rym: are people going to be showered and what does the sleeping and power situation look like.

471
00:31:54,162 --> 00:31:57,700
Rym: I've spent about 25 minutes looking into the details of this con.

472
00:31:58,480 --> 00:32:00,146
Scott: It just seems like logistically difficult, right?

473
00:32:00,166 --> 00:32:02,716
Scott: You have to make a mega Costco run before, right?

474
00:32:02,816 --> 00:32:03,700
Scott: Bring everything there.

475
00:32:04,300 --> 00:32:06,524
Rym: Or just sign up for that.

476
00:32:06,805 --> 00:32:15,160
Rym: I saw some photos of the dining hall's food and it looked like it was about twice as good as what I remember band camp food to be.

477
00:32:15,561 --> 00:32:19,798
Rym: Which is above the line of acceptable but is just shy of good.

478
00:32:22,082 --> 00:32:24,140
Scott: I've eaten much summer camp food in my life.

479
00:32:26,244 --> 00:32:27,309
Rym: Alright, let's get right to the main bit.

480
00:32:27,429 --> 00:32:29,539
Rym: Emily and I watched the show quite some time ago.

481
00:32:30,600 --> 00:32:33,676
Rym: Scott watched it recently so we can now talk about it.

482
00:32:33,797 --> 00:32:34,400
Rym: It is the anime.

483
00:32:35,261 --> 00:32:39,640
Scott: I'm trying to get through Netflix so I can watch all the stuff on it and then stop paying for it.

484
00:32:40,600 --> 00:32:41,585
Scott: So that was on my list.

485
00:32:41,887 --> 00:32:43,193
Scott: I got around to it eventually.

486
00:32:43,455 --> 00:32:44,500
Scott: I watch things eventually.

487
00:32:44,960 --> 00:32:47,680
Rym: I'm pretty sure I deep-fited it some time ago but The Orbital Children.

488
00:32:47,740 --> 00:32:49,348
Scott: There's no rush, right?

489
00:32:49,429 --> 00:32:51,539
Scott: It's a shame show that it was when it was new.

490
00:32:52,223 --> 00:32:53,432
Scott: I saw it before I died.

491
00:32:53,815 --> 00:32:54,359
Scott: What's the big deal?

492
00:32:55,142 --> 00:32:58,456
Rym: I guess the important things about it were that one, it's on Netflix.

493
00:32:58,516 --> 00:33:00,143
Rym: It's from 2022.

494
00:33:00,143 --> 00:33:01,399
Rym: So it's a relatively recent anime.

495
00:33:02,120 --> 00:33:09,560
Rym: Two, it is a fairly hard sci-fi compared to a lot of anime that are set in space like, say, Gundams.

496
00:33:11,202 --> 00:33:18,200
Rym: It does deal with some pretty direct, serious science fiction concepts in a serious, direct, and interesting way.

497
00:33:19,081 --> 00:33:22,100
Rym: It was written and directed by Mitsuo Iso.

498
00:33:23,662 --> 00:33:26,360
Rym: Not to get into his whole history because I didn't know a lot about him.

499
00:33:26,460 --> 00:33:33,740
Rym: But if you go to his Wikipedia and you look at what he's worked on, it's that golden type of person to make a good anime.

500
00:33:34,241 --> 00:33:44,180
Rym: He has mostly done key animation for important anime over history like End of Evangelion, Perfect Blue, Cowboy Bebop.

501
00:33:46,481 --> 00:33:51,080
Rym: So the character designs were good by a person you might all recognize.

502
00:33:53,102 --> 00:33:54,868
Rym: This came out of him and Deno Coil.

503
00:33:54,949 --> 00:33:57,959
Rym: So there's a lot of reasons to check this show out.

504
00:33:59,842 --> 00:34:01,740
Scott: And it's also only, what, six episodes?

505
00:34:01,860 --> 00:34:02,944
Rym: Yeah, it's not super long.

506
00:34:03,084 --> 00:34:05,372
Rym: It doesn't fuck around.

507
00:34:05,673 --> 00:34:06,756
Rym: It introduces the world.

508
00:34:06,816 --> 00:34:07,880
Rym: It introduces the concept.

509
00:34:08,960 --> 00:34:13,190
Rym: The inciting incident happens basically immediately.

510
00:34:13,210 --> 00:34:17,120
Rym: The mystery is introduced basically immediately.

511
00:34:17,581 --> 00:34:23,320
Rym: And by the end of this short show, every question is answered, everything is resolved, and it's very satisfying.

512
00:34:25,484 --> 00:34:28,835
Scott: Yeah, so you've got Orbital Children, obviously, just based on the title.

513
00:34:28,875 --> 00:34:30,199
Scott: It's like, "Oh, this is going to be about kids in

514
00:34:30,219 --> 00:34:30,420
Scott: space.".

515
00:34:30,620 --> 00:34:33,828
Scott: And yeah, it's about kids in space.

516
00:34:34,168 --> 00:34:38,420
Scott: And it's about a lot of concepts that you've seen in kids in space animes before.

517
00:34:38,840 --> 00:34:45,460
Scott: Especially Gundam, where you've got the people who live in space against the people who live on Earth and the conflicts between them.

518
00:34:46,000 --> 00:34:50,658
Scott: And the fate of humanity, the destiny of the human race, etc.

519
00:34:52,481 --> 00:34:57,409
Rym: It's got the typical discussion of, "Is AI good?

520
00:34:57,590 --> 00:34:58,792
Rym: Is AI bad?

521
00:34:58,892 --> 00:35:02,378
Rym: Is AI just a very complicated and difficult thing for humans to deal

522
00:35:02,438 --> 00:35:02,598
Rym: with?".

523
00:35:05,081 --> 00:35:16,879
Scott: And it's also got sort of the 2001 aspect of like, "Ah, yes, this is the space station, and this is space tourism, and people can just come up to space if they want

524
00:35:16,899 --> 00:35:17,039
Scott: to.".

525
00:35:19,180 --> 00:35:20,343
Scott: What's that like?

526
00:35:20,724 --> 00:35:25,435
Scott: And also, criticisms of Earth government structures, right?

527
00:35:25,455 --> 00:35:26,016
Scott: It's like, "It's

528
00:35:26,036 --> 00:35:26,097
Scott: UN2!".

529
00:35:28,163 --> 00:35:31,657
Scott: Yeah, the UN1, it failed, and now we have UN2 to find out.

530
00:35:32,540 --> 00:35:37,100
Rym: But it's also something that I think is worth calling out, because I didn't think about it until watching the show.

531
00:35:37,940 --> 00:35:51,140
Rym: Generally, the best science fiction that I've read or watched over the years will have many science fiction and speculative concepts in it, but will primarily focus on a smaller number of them for its deep analysis.

532
00:35:51,740 --> 00:36:00,400
Rym: But it will have the others there, treated accurately, interestingly, and appropriately, even if it's not directly interrogating them.

533
00:36:01,261 --> 00:36:11,920
Rym: And sci-fi that is the top tier, S tier, tends to have all that there in the background, which gives you a more complex and holistic speculative world, but doesn't super delve on them.

534
00:36:12,260 --> 00:36:24,119
Rym: Like, worst science fiction addresses literally every concept fully and tends to be tedious, and Ray Bradbury tends to just pick, "We're going to look at exactly one thing for 20 pages, and that's it.

535
00:36:24,239 --> 00:36:24,639
Rym: Nothing else

536
00:36:24,700 --> 00:36:24,960
Rym: exists.".

537
00:36:25,823 --> 00:36:30,638
Scott: Yeah, here, it's like, you see some concepts that are real and also not real.

538
00:36:32,160 --> 00:36:49,060
Scott: But it's like, there was an episode where a good most of the episode was focused on just the danger of being in space, running out of oxygen, being sucked into the vacuum, dying, depressurizing, all those sorts of normal, hard sci-fi things that happen every time someone's in space.

539
00:36:50,361 --> 00:36:51,883
Scott: But is the show about that?

540
00:36:52,323 --> 00:36:53,384
Scott: Not at all, really.

541
00:36:54,485 --> 00:37:10,360
Scott: But it is sort of necessary to sort of lock the setting in place, force the characters into the right situations, and force the relationships between the characters into certain arrangements.

542
00:37:11,300 --> 00:37:21,260
Scott: Because, you know, regardless of their differences, they have to work together to survive in this part, so that way you can get past that, and so that the rest of the show can continue.

543
00:37:21,440 --> 00:37:31,000
Scott: Because if they were just perfectly safe and weren't forced to work together, then the show would have just been the direct conflict between the characters and their differences who wins, the end.

544
00:37:31,160 --> 00:37:39,720
Rym: Now, interesting, because one of the concepts of the show might be, humans, if not put under pressure and forced to work together, won't solve any of these problems.

545
00:37:40,600 --> 00:37:40,850
Scott: Could be.

546
00:37:42,100 --> 00:37:45,706
Rym: Yeah, I mean, it's a short show, so we're not gonna spoil super details of it.

547
00:37:45,787 --> 00:37:47,129
Rym: I would recommend watching it.

548
00:37:47,189 --> 00:37:53,620
Rym: I think anyone who listens to GeekNights, regardless of if you like anime, would enjoy this show, unless you hate science fiction.

549
00:37:55,261 --> 00:38:02,714
Scott: Yeah, I mean, it's not the best show I've ever seen, but I watched a couple episodes, and I'm like, "Oh, there's only four.

550
00:38:02,774 --> 00:38:03,395
Scott: Might as well.

551
00:38:04,618 --> 00:38:05,519
Scott: Good stuff's going on

552
00:38:05,559 --> 00:38:05,700
Scott: here.".

553
00:38:05,940 --> 00:38:16,500
Rym: So the premise where it starts, also, it hits the ground running, because it basically, it definitely implies that Earth is a capitalist, dystopia-adjacent thing.

554
00:38:17,261 --> 00:38:19,846
Rym: The kids are up in space, but some of them are subsidized.

555
00:38:20,107 --> 00:38:23,774
Rym: Some of them are clearly influencers who are part of an advertising campaign.

556
00:38:24,295 --> 00:38:27,180
Rym: There's ads everywhere in the space station.

557
00:38:27,680 --> 00:38:34,300
Rym: At one point, I remember one of them, they used a mascot suit as an evac suit, because that's the only thing they had around.

558
00:38:34,940 --> 00:38:35,744
Scott: That's the old man.

559
00:38:35,765 --> 00:38:38,620
Scott: That's the old man who designed the station.

560
00:38:39,381 --> 00:38:41,869
Rym: Not just him.

561
00:38:41,990 --> 00:38:45,040
Rym: There's the weird pink suit that the one girl puts on at one point.

562
00:38:45,401 --> 00:38:56,577
Scott: Oh, that's just the tutorial suit, because people don't know how to... Because the station was set up as a place for tourists, when they're trying to survive, it's like, "Ah, yes.

563
00:38:56,958 --> 00:38:58,039
Scott: We'll have to use these...".

564
00:38:58,961 --> 00:39:05,780
Scott: They make use of the tourist equipment that was meant to be a tourist attraction as survival equipment.

565
00:39:06,081 --> 00:39:13,700
Scott: So there's an EVAC, an EVA attraction, where tourists would go out and do a spacewalk.

566
00:39:14,561 --> 00:39:20,338
Scott: Well, it's like, "Oh, we need to use the tourist spacewalk suits to go out, because we don't have anything

567
00:39:20,458 --> 00:39:20,619
Scott: else.".

568
00:39:20,980 --> 00:39:23,250
Scott: And one of them is just a ball meant to put children into.

569
00:39:26,241 --> 00:39:34,320
Scott: One of them is a tutorial one that does everything for you, and then there's some real ones that they have.

570
00:39:35,261 --> 00:39:44,920
Rym: But basically, the premise, where it starts is you've got this influencer, you've got some kids, kids who were born in space versus kids who were born on Earth and the differences between them.

571
00:39:45,400 --> 00:39:50,260
Rym: You've got genetically engineered kids versus less genetically engineered or normal kids.

572
00:39:50,300 --> 00:40:00,860
Scott: I don't think they were genetically engineered, but because they were born in space, they had these implants put into them so that they could survive in space, but the implants were also a problem.

573
00:40:01,301 --> 00:40:03,329
Scott: And that's basically the main plot.

574
00:40:06,920 --> 00:40:18,540
Rym: So it sets all these kids up, people are on the station, things are going fine, and then a bunch of stuff happens at once, and you only see things from the perspective of the people on the space station.

575
00:40:18,700 --> 00:40:28,440
Rym: So imagine you're up on a space station, and then there's weird radio chatter from Earth, and then a nuke gets launched, and you don't know where that nuke is targeted.

576
00:40:28,560 --> 00:40:38,120
Rym: You just find out, yeah, Earth just launched a bunch of nukes somewhere, and then a comet or some debris or something hits your space station and cuts off communication.

577
00:40:38,460 --> 00:40:47,680
Rym: So now you're on a space station, all the emergency systems are in effect, you've lost contact with Earth, and all you know is a bunch of nukes went off right before this happened.

578
00:40:48,361 --> 00:40:49,378
Rym: What the fuck do you do?

579
00:40:50,721 --> 00:40:54,045
Scott: I think that's probably actually my favorite aspect of the show, right?

580
00:40:54,065 --> 00:41:06,340
Scott: There's too many things out there, stories, artworks, whatever, where they show something from multiple perspectives to sort of give you the whole picture of what's going on.

581
00:41:07,601 --> 00:41:14,239
Scott: Or even if they don't do it immediately, they can't hold back, and halfway through they're like, "Okay, here's what the other side is,

582
00:41:14,299 --> 00:41:14,600
Scott: guys.".

583
00:41:15,621 --> 00:41:19,300
Scott: It's like, to hold the whole show to the edge.

584
00:41:19,360 --> 00:41:22,640
Rym: Yeah, you don't find out what was up on Earth until the end of the show.

585
00:41:23,421 --> 00:41:34,900
Scott: Right, it's like you literally throughout the whole show only know what, even on Earth at the end of the show, you only know what these characters that are the main characters know.

586
00:41:35,020 --> 00:41:36,479
Scott: You don't know anything else, ever.

587
00:41:37,160 --> 00:41:41,720
Scott: There are several characters, so it's not like a Half-Life 2 where you only know what Gordon Freeman knows.

588
00:41:42,161 --> 00:41:44,023
Scott: It's not limited to that extent, but it's like.

589
00:41:44,323 --> 00:41:57,500
Scott: the only people on the space station are the two kids that live there, the woman that takes care of them, the three tourist kids that come up, the old man who designed the station and is in a mascot suit.

590
00:41:58,041 --> 00:42:08,377
Scott: And then there's three people who are the commander and the co-pilots of the space station, but even them, they're sort of off to the side.

591
00:42:08,477 --> 00:42:10,000
Scott: It's mostly that first group.

592
00:42:10,960 --> 00:42:14,849
Scott: And the old man is really just sort of like, he might as well be the cute animal character.

593
00:42:14,869 --> 00:42:18,177
Scott: I think that's why they put him in the mascot suit.

594
00:42:18,598 --> 00:42:19,540
Scott: He's sort of like the Pikachu.

595
00:42:21,541 --> 00:42:22,904
Scott: He doesn't really count that much.

596
00:42:22,944 --> 00:42:24,407
Scott: He only really comes into play.

597
00:42:24,968 --> 00:42:31,200
Scott: He's actually mostly in play in the first episode and then one other time, and then they sort of hide him off to the side.

598
00:42:31,580 --> 00:42:34,405
Scott: And I'm like, I think that guy's more significant.

599
00:42:34,425 --> 00:42:42,760
Scott: You said he designed most of the station, but he's old and has sort of this Alzheimer's that comes back and comes in and out.

600
00:42:42,900 --> 00:42:46,946
Scott: And sometimes he can't remember things and then suddenly he's just fine other times.

601
00:42:47,387 --> 00:42:49,930
Scott: And then they just sort of forget about him at some point.

602
00:42:50,010 --> 00:42:50,791
Scott: It's like, what the hell?

603
00:42:52,334 --> 00:42:53,115
Scott: Maybe not do that?

604
00:42:53,575 --> 00:42:53,976
Scott: I don't know.

605
00:42:53,996 --> 00:42:56,720
Scott: I want to know more about that old man.

606
00:42:57,441 --> 00:43:00,339
Rym: I guess they had more pressing concerns like human instrumentality.

607
00:43:01,342 --> 00:43:02,159
Scott: I guess so, yeah.

608
00:43:02,200 --> 00:43:14,240
Rym: Because what's interesting is, again, having recently watched Pluto, I would say one of the main themes of this show that it addresses pretty much head on is the same core idea that Pluto deals with.

609
00:43:15,180 --> 00:43:25,696
Rym: If there's going to be AIs, restricting them versus not restricting them is a binary choice that has extreme consequences in both directions.

610
00:43:26,117 --> 00:43:28,200
Rym: That's a core theme of this.

611
00:43:29,301 --> 00:43:35,370
Scott: But they seem to like basically the humans in this world have like decided, you know, clearly AIs are people, right?

612
00:43:35,390 --> 00:43:35,811
Scott: They haven't.

613
00:43:36,231 --> 00:43:37,633
Scott: It's like it's clear, right?

614
00:43:37,674 --> 00:43:41,880
Scott: There isn't like this debate about it, you know, like you would have an older sci fi.

615
00:43:42,260 --> 00:43:42,481
Scott: Right.

616
00:43:42,861 --> 00:43:51,400
Scott: But here it's like there's like they're restricting the cognition of the AIs because if the AIs get too smart, it'll be like a Skynet situation.

617
00:43:51,961 --> 00:44:04,960
Rym: Partly because they had an AI, and this is revealed pretty early in the show, that is like a case study that the UN got involved with because it was really smart and it got weird and possibly dangerous.

618
00:44:05,160 --> 00:44:15,255
Rym: And they had to shut it down because it basically just said to its handlers, "Yeah, we're going to reduce Earth's population by X

619
00:44:15,295 --> 00:44:15,696
Rym: percent.".

620
00:44:15,836 --> 00:44:18,720
Rym: And then it seemed to try to execute on that mission.

621
00:44:19,541 --> 00:44:20,764
Scott: Yeah.

622
00:44:21,767 --> 00:44:26,080
Scott: So obviously, right, they actually managed to avert disaster in their eyes.

623
00:44:26,480 --> 00:44:31,140
Rym: So now they've got restrictions on AI and like the UN has a whole thing about AI and that is central to the plot.

624
00:44:31,340 --> 00:44:31,440
Scott: Yeah.

625
00:44:31,460 --> 00:44:32,001
Scott: It's like.

626
00:44:32,021 --> 00:44:43,640
Scott: it's like the AIs are smart or smarter than people and the people work in concert with them, but they don't let the AIs become ultra smart because the one that did become ultra smart basically sort of became godlike.

627
00:44:43,740 --> 00:44:51,800
Scott: Like, you know, people like worship it and like they have like this like they talk about like the poem that the AI that got out of control was like this called seven.

628
00:44:52,520 --> 00:44:58,320
Scott: And they're like, oh, yeah, the seven poem is sort of like the Bible or like a Nostradamus thing that predicts the future.

629
00:44:58,520 --> 00:45:07,360
Rym: Now, I'm going to say I think we're going to see actual humans in real life act like that in regard to some machine learning model within the next three or four years.

630
00:45:08,640 --> 00:45:08,921
Scott: Maybe.

631
00:45:08,981 --> 00:45:10,202
Scott: I don't know.

632
00:45:10,943 --> 00:45:16,668
Scott: But it's like, yeah, it's like what if there was an AI that wasn't just some bullshit human mimic thing like we have now?

633
00:45:16,869 --> 00:45:27,760
Scott: that isn't, you know, I would argue in a lot of cases isn't AI, but is actually a general intelligence and is actually way, way smarter than we are.

634
00:45:28,220 --> 00:45:28,501
Scott: Right.

635
00:45:28,621 --> 00:45:29,142
Scott: Yeah.

636
00:45:29,182 --> 00:45:29,643
Scott: Think about it.

637
00:45:29,703 --> 00:45:30,384
Scott: We starts.

638
00:45:30,825 --> 00:45:30,965
Scott: Right.

639
00:45:31,025 --> 00:45:32,669
Scott: And it starts doing things.

640
00:45:32,869 --> 00:45:33,891
Scott: It's like, should we trust it?

641
00:45:35,133 --> 00:45:38,800
Scott: And, you know, and be it like developed in this world.

642
00:45:39,140 --> 00:45:47,080
Scott: One of the big questions that the show asks is there was a lot of technology developed by the godlike AI and no one understands it.

643
00:45:47,180 --> 00:45:51,940
Scott: And because all the other eyes are limited, those other eyes don't understand that technology either.

644
00:45:52,421 --> 00:46:05,580
Scott: So anything they still use the, some of the really useful things that old AI made and right, sort of like how in Macross, they have the ship that's from the, oh yeah, it is like Macross, right?

645
00:46:05,740 --> 00:46:09,688
Scott: The Macross is basically a spaceship that comes from, right?

646
00:46:09,808 --> 00:46:10,770
Scott: The what's it called?

647
00:46:11,511 --> 00:46:16,120
Scott: The surveyor, the whatever the, I forget the name of the alien race that made the Macross.

648
00:46:16,200 --> 00:46:18,885
Scott: It's not the Zentradi who may, who are attacking the man.

649
00:46:19,967 --> 00:46:20,949
Scott: Oh, the supervisor.

650
00:46:21,109 --> 00:46:26,820
Scott: That's who I think is the supervisor military anyway, but the Macross itself was made by this other alien race.

651
00:46:27,121 --> 00:46:33,520
Scott: They fix it up and they learn about it, but like they don't really, you know, it's, it's, they're using unknown stuff.

652
00:46:33,620 --> 00:46:46,423
Scott: So what if instead of aliens bringing unknown stuff and us using it and AI makes unknown stuff and now that AI is gone and no one can repair or work on or understand that technology, but you're still using it, but

653
00:46:46,463 --> 00:46:53,478
Rym: also everyone side eyes it a little bit because everyone thinks back to what the crazy AI that they shut down did and said, right?

654
00:46:55,100 --> 00:47:05,160
Scott: Like imagine if I went back in time to like the early 20th century with just like a box of iPhones and a cell tower and then, and then went away just like, well, how would that go?

655
00:47:06,422 --> 00:47:16,440
Rym: And I guess the other thing that this hits pretty head on is a concept in not, not like Evangelion instrumentality, but more about the concept of a technological singularity.

656
00:47:17,641 --> 00:47:20,946
Rym: Is there a difference between humans and humanity?

657
00:47:21,567 --> 00:47:22,950
Rym: Does that difference matter?

658
00:47:23,470 --> 00:47:25,053
Rym: And are they both important?

659
00:47:25,494 --> 00:47:29,300
Rym: And it answers those questions in a very nuanced way.

660
00:47:29,620 --> 00:47:32,344
Rym: Like it, it's a, watch the show.

661
00:47:32,404 --> 00:47:35,107
Rym: I think it addresses it pretty well and I think it'll leave you thinking.

662
00:47:35,668 --> 00:47:46,060
Rym: And I don't think it has a very strong conclusion about everything, but I think it's going to introduce a lot of food for thought if you've never read much about what the singularity might entail.

663
00:47:47,681 --> 00:47:57,880
Scott: So, you know, if you like a sci-fi anime and a terrible secret of space or terrible secret of earth or whatever, um, I guess it's not a secret of space even though it's in space.

664
00:47:58,100 --> 00:48:07,300
Rym: But if you very specifically, if you've been burned by media that doesn't just answer what the secret is by the end, this one answers it at the end.

665
00:48:07,661 --> 00:48:10,407
Scott: I don't think I, I would on that point, I would differ.

666
00:48:10,728 --> 00:48:15,680
Scott: I would say I don't like it when the secret is just, you know, revealed.

667
00:48:16,082 --> 00:48:18,060
Rym: In this case I do only because.

668
00:48:19,120 --> 00:48:31,920
Rym: Well cause the show here is specifically, the quest, answering the secret opens the new question of, alright humans, your move, what do you do with that information?

669
00:48:33,280 --> 00:48:37,330
Scott: Right, it's like everyone loves that stupid, you know, Christopher Nolan guy, right?

670
00:48:37,431 --> 00:48:40,098
Scott: But like, what was the space one he did, I saw?

671
00:48:41,901 --> 00:48:43,376
Rym: Oh yeah, I actually didn't see it.

672
00:48:44,882 --> 00:48:48,099
Rym: Uh, I mean you might like it but I'll watch it at some point, I know I'll like it.

673
00:48:48,740 --> 00:48:49,660
Scott: Uh, what the hell?

674
00:48:49,660 --> 00:48:50,940
Scott: I like tedious space nonsense.

675
00:48:52,060 --> 00:48:53,879
Scott: Uh, Interstellar by that one.

676
00:48:54,200 --> 00:48:57,026
Scott: Where it's like, yeah, it's got a terrible secret of space.

677
00:48:57,146 --> 00:49:03,260
Scott: and then at the end they explain the entire terrible secret of space explicitly, they're like this is what it was.

678
00:49:03,500 --> 00:49:04,400
Rym: Yeah, well I guess I don't.

679
00:49:04,601 --> 00:49:07,798
Scott: I'm just like, eh, eh, it's overrated.

680
00:49:08,140 --> 00:49:18,660
Rym: Anyway, good media that has a terrible secret of space either has to answer it in a way that is actually satisfying and lives up to whatever you were thinking, which is very hard to do.

681
00:49:19,702 --> 00:49:23,740
Rym: Or answering it opens another even more interesting question.

682
00:49:24,621 --> 00:49:29,692
Rym: Or doesn't answer it, usually because the point of the show is more like Evangelion around.

683
00:49:30,073 --> 00:49:33,080
Rym: that question was stupid, here's a different question you should have been asking.

684
00:49:34,121 --> 00:49:41,920
Scott: Eh, I think still the best terrible secret of space movie ever is still 2001, which is just like, ah fuck it, here's some psychedelic imagery dishes.

685
00:49:42,561 --> 00:49:45,120
Scott: And that is vastly superior, right?

686
00:49:45,140 --> 00:49:53,920
Rym: But was the answer also not pretty direct of, oh, they took a human and decided to uplift them just like what the monolith they showed you in the beginning.

687
00:49:54,461 --> 00:49:58,399
Rym: And here's a psychedelic nonsense way to portray that, but that's what it is.

688
00:49:59,162 --> 00:50:05,480
Scott: That's uninterpretation, but I think that's by just showing abstract imagery of that nature, right?

689
00:50:06,020 --> 00:50:13,300
Scott: And just sort of, they leave you to your own interpretation, there is no right or wrong answer of what actually happened, right?

690
00:50:14,281 --> 00:50:28,040
Scott: And when you give an answer, you're not leaving that space for the viewer to, the audience to be challenged and also to have to look inside themselves for an answer, right?

691
00:50:28,302 --> 00:50:30,400
Scott: Instead of seeking out an answer from elsewhere.

692
00:50:30,720 --> 00:50:35,540
Scott: Well I guess in this case then, that's part of why- In 2001, you only can get answers from yourself.

693
00:50:36,140 --> 00:50:46,456
Rym: I guess then why this works here for Orbital Children to take it from that angle is that when you find out like what happened, that makes you ask questions.

694
00:50:46,496 --> 00:50:49,200
Rym: you weren't thinking about asking up to this point in the show.

695
00:50:49,841 --> 00:50:50,021
Scott: Yeah.

696
00:50:50,422 --> 00:51:00,700
Scott: It's not bad, but I'm just saying it's, you know, if you like that sort of thing, you can get it in six episodes, which I think is a great lesson for everyone out there who takes fucking 26 plus episodes to do this.

697
00:51:01,480 --> 00:51:03,903
Scott: It's much better when you do it in less, right?

698
00:51:04,163 --> 00:51:08,108
Scott: You don't actually need to build up, you know, the whole time, right?

699
00:51:08,168 --> 00:51:18,660
Scott: If you're not using that time valuably, you can just do it in, you know, if your finale of your show is, you know, you're going to start getting terrible secretly in the last two or three episodes.

700
00:51:19,301 --> 00:51:22,829
Scott: You don't need more than two or three episodes to set up before that of the normal part.

701
00:51:22,869 --> 00:51:26,096
Scott: You don't need 20 episodes normal part, right?

702
00:51:26,657 --> 00:51:27,780
Scott: Six episodes weird part.

703
00:51:28,100 --> 00:51:29,022
Scott: We can do three and three.

704
00:51:29,423 --> 00:51:30,505
Scott: It's fine, right?

705
00:51:30,525 --> 00:51:31,347
Scott: Don't waste our time.

706
00:51:31,687 --> 00:51:33,050
Scott: That I can watch more shows that way.

707
00:51:33,491 --> 00:51:34,192
Scott: It's on Netflix.

708
00:51:34,212 --> 00:51:37,178
Scott: You don't need to make a whole TV season for television.

709
00:51:37,198 --> 00:51:38,200
Scott: No one watches that anymore.

710
00:51:39,140 --> 00:51:40,847
Scott: You'll save money on production.

711
00:51:42,112 --> 00:51:42,634
Scott: It's fine.

712
00:51:43,517 --> 00:51:44,200
Scott: Just do this instead.

713
00:51:49,733 --> 00:51:52,121
Rym: This has been GeekNights with Rym and Scott.

714
00:51:52,161 --> 00:51:54,690
Rym: Special thanks to DJ Pretzel for the opening theme.

715
00:51:54,890 --> 00:52:00,910
Scott: Be sure to visit our website at FrontRowClue.com for show notes, discussion, links and more.

716
00:52:01,410 --> 00:52:04,055
Rym: Remember, GeekNights is not one, but four different shows.

717
00:52:04,375 --> 00:52:06,018
Rym: Mondays are science and technology.

718
00:52:06,078 --> 00:52:07,821
Rym: Tuesdays, gaming and games.

719
00:52:07,942 --> 00:52:09,765
Rym: Wednesdays are animation and comics.

720
00:52:10,086 --> 00:52:12,570
Rym: Thursdays are arbitrary and indiscriminate.

721
00:52:13,230 --> 00:52:18,050
Rym: GeekNights is distributed under a Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International License.

722
00:52:18,571 --> 00:52:22,941
Scott: GeekNights is recorded live with no studio and no audience.

723
00:52:23,422 --> 00:52:26,890
Scott: But unlike those other late shows, it's actually recorded at night.

724
00:52:30,130 --> 00:52:32,333
Rym: Anyway, tonight I will leave you with something you might not have expected.

725
00:52:45,351 --> 00:52:47,674
Rym: Lyrics coming at you at supersonic speed.

726
00:53:01,631 --> 00:53:03,294
Scott: Boomhauer, I didn't understand a word you just said.

